 Thus an EMA-rate proposal mode follows if the resonance forcing has multiplier \(\alpha\) and the branch contraction is uniformly faster than \(\alpha\). This rate ordering is replay evidence and is not assumed in Theorem 6.2.
 
+### Proposition 7.1 — asymptotic forcing-transfer gain
+
+Assume for one channel that
+
+\[
+\lambda_n\to\lambda_*,
+\qquad
+\frac{r_{n+1}}{r_n}\to\alpha,
+\qquad
+r_n>0
+\]
+
+eventually, with
+
+\[
+0\le\lambda_*<\alpha<1.
+\]
+
+Then
+
+\[
+\boxed{
+\frac{d_n}{r_n}
+\to
+G_*
+=
+\frac{a_R\alpha}{\alpha-\lambda_*}.
+}
+\]
+
+### Proof
+
+Set \(g_n=d_n/r_n\). The exact forced recurrence gives
+
+\[
+g_{n+1}
+=
+\lambda_n\frac{r_n}{r_{n+1}}g_n+a_R.
+\]
+
+Its multiplier converges to \(\lambda_*/\alpha<1\). The resulting asymptotically contractive affine recurrence converges to
+
+\[
+G_*
+=
+\frac{a_R}{1-\lambda_*/\alpha}
+=
+\frac{a_R\alpha}{\alpha-\lambda_*}.
+\]
+
+∎
+
+The gain is an open-loop stable-filter transfer ratio on the fixed branch. It is not a full delayed-system loop gain.
+
